External model-service access
============================

No private infrastructure access is required, and the authors do not operate a
hosted endpoint for reviewers. This artifact distributes no model weights, no
inference service, and no credentials. The evaluator supplies the LLM endpoint.

There are two supported ways to do that, and either one satisfies every claim in
claims/. Pick whichever matches the hardware you already have.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
OPTION A -- Self-hosted vLLM (the paper-reference path)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Use this if you have administrative access to an NVIDIA GPU server. It is the
configuration the paper's measurements were produced with, and it keeps the
report text on your own hardware, which is the privacy property paper
Challenge 3 is about.

    See VLLM_SETUP.md for the full end-to-end procedure: prerequisites, image
    preparation, the pinned model snapshot, the launch command, readiness
    checks, and troubleshooting.

Summary:

    ./infrastructure/prepare_vllm_node.sh     # build/obtain the vllm-node image
    ./infrastructure/start_vllm_qwen35.sh     # launch the pinned Qwen snapshot
    curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8081/v1/models # confirm readiness

Then, in artifact/.env:

    LLM_PROVIDER=vllm
    VLLM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8081/v1
    VLLM_API_KEY=dummy
    VLLM_LLM_MODEL=qwen3.5-35b

Any OpenAI-compatible server that supports tool calling works here, not only
this model and not only this image. A smaller open-weights model on a modest GPU
is fine for the functional claims, provided it sustains multi-step tool calling
-- see the note on model capability below. Set VLLM_LLM_MODEL to whatever name
your server serves.

Servers that reject non-OpenAI body fields are handled automatically: the
thinking toggle uses vLLM's `chat_template_kwargs` extension, and if the
endpoint answers HTTP 400 for it, ANCHOR warns once, drops the field, and
retries. Tolerant servers ignore the field and need no change, though on those
the VLLM_THINKING toggle has no effect and a reasoning model may exhaust
LLM_MAX_TOKENS on thinking tokens; raise that limit if output is truncated.
VLLM_EXTRA_BODY=false suppresses the field from the start. If ANCHOR runs on a different machine from the
server, replace 127.0.0.1 with that server's address and protect the endpoint
with your normal VPN, firewall, or SSH-tunnel policy.

Reference hardware for self-hosting Qwen3.5-35B-A3B-FP8 is in
infrastructure/resources and HARDWARE_REQUIREMENTS.md. The pinned revision and
per-shard checksums are in infrastructure/model_snapshot.json.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
OPTION B -- A hosted API (no GPU required)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Use this if you do not have a GPU server. ANCHOR resolves its chat model at
runtime from LLM_PROVIDER (artifact/src/utils/llm.py), so a hosted endpoint
drives the identical pipeline from a commodity laptop.

In artifact/.env, set ONE of:

    LLM_PROVIDER=openai
    OPENAI_API_KEY=<your key>
    OPENAI_LLM_MODEL=gpt-4o

    -- or --

    LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic
    ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=<your key>
    ANTHROPIC_LLM_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-6

This sends report text to a third-party API. The packaged CTINexus reports are
public threat reports, so that is not a disclosure concern for this evaluation.
It does mean Option B does not exercise the local-LLM privacy property of paper
Challenge 3 -- only Option A does. It exercises everything else.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIGURATION AND VERIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    cp artifact/.env.example artifact/.env

artifact/.env.example is a dotfile; it is present in the repository and in the
release archive even though `ls` hides it by default. Use `ls -a artifact/`.
Fill in only the block for the provider you chose. Never commit artifact/.env;
it is git-ignored.

Verify the configured endpoint before running a claim:

    artifact/.venv/bin/python artifact/scripts/check_endpoint.py

It prints the provider, base URL, and model it resolved, and reports an
actionable error if the endpoint is unreachable, the key is missing or rejected,
or the configured model is not served -- in which case it lists the names the
server does serve. No model name is pinned by the check.

It then asks the model to make one tool call. ANCHOR's ontology mapper depends
on multi-step tool use, so a model without it completes a run but yields an
empty graph after several minutes; this probe surfaces that in seconds. It is
advisory -- a WARNING, never a hard failure, since a capable model may decline a
single prompt. ANCHOR_SKIP_TOOL_PROBE=1 skips it.

claims/claim3 requires no endpoint at all and can be run immediately after
./install.sh.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECRETS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

No reusable secret, private IP address, or author-only endpoint is embedded in
this package or its release archive.
